--TEST--
The four shapes the primer program is built out of, each broken one way: a knob passed as a parameter name, a free function, a binding given a second type, and a `foreach` binding with none (`rule:core-api/options-bag`, `rule:classes/no-free-functions-or-constants`, `rule:types/declaration`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:programs/a-complete-program-annotated
array<string> $amounts = ["3", "11", "7"];

// `rule:core-api/options-bag`: a knob is a key inside the one trailing
// literal, never a parameter of its own, so there is no parameter named
// `order` for this to fill.
array<string> $ordered = Core\Arr::sort($amounts, order: Core\Order::Asc);

// `rule:classes/no-free-functions-or-constants`: every callable is a member of
// a class, so the PHP habit finds no name to call.
echo count($amounts), "\n";

// `rule:types/declaration`: the type is declared once and never changes, so a
// `string` cannot be written into a name declared `int`.
int $total = 0;
$total = "seven";

// `rule:types/declaration`: the binding a `foreach` takes out is declared the
// same way, and leaving the type off is not a shorter spelling of it.
foreach ($amounts as $each) {
    echo $each, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0486]: no parameter of this call can be filled by the name `order`
%A
error[E0320]: `count` is not a function that exists
%A
error[E0401]: expected `int`, found `string`
%A
error[E0101]: expected a `foreach` binding's type
%A
error: aborting due to 4 errors
